  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Lake Saint-Cassien is a 420-hectare reservoir lake, 7 km long and 3.5 km wide, straddling Montauroux, Callian, Tanneron and Les Adrets-de-l'Estérel, in the Var next to the Alpes-Maritimes. It is part of a chain of hydroelectric developments and also serves to share the water resource. The Pays de Fayence community of communes manages the tourist facilities there: a team cleans and clears the shores all year, with a lifeguard post and an eco-guard in season.</p>

      <h2>The rule for dogs</h2>
      <p>The Pays de Fayence tourist office writes it plainly: animals are allowed on the beach, on a leash, but <strong>swimming is banned for any animal, even on a leash</strong>. Its lake brochure lists "animals running free" among the banned things (allowed on the beaches, but on a leash) and, for animals swimming, cites the municipal orders of Montauroux (16/04/2015) and Tanneron (20/04/2015). Its dog-tourism guide (May 2025) sums up: dogs on a leash are allowed on the beaches but banned from swimming. On the Fondurane biotope, as on the Natura 2000 sites of the Siagne gorges, a leash is compulsory.</p>

      <h2>On site with your dog</h2>
      <p>The supervised Tanneron beach is below the Maison du Lac (RD 37): free, with parking, picnic tables and an access ramp. It is supervised on weekends in June, every day from 1 July to 31 August and on weekends in September, 11am to 7pm. Elsewhere, the area most often mentioned for spreading a towel with a leashed dog is the Plage de l'Américain, chemin du Gabinet in Montauroux, or the Pré Claou beach, just before the bridge. Pine woods line some shores and give shade in summer. Parking is free in the designated areas. The brochure also bans fires, stoves, bivouacking and camper-van camping: plan a cold meal and water for your dog, who will not be able to cool off in the lake.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, choose the morning for the walk along the shores, avoid the hot hours and keep the water bowl within reach, since the lake remains off-limits for dog swimming.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Pays de Fayence, just above the lake, has other waterways where dog swimming is possible, such as the <a href="/en/fiche-gorges-blavet">Gorges du Blavet</a> or the <a href="/en/fiche-pont-mons-siagnole">Pont de Mons on the Siagnole</a>. Towards Tanneron, the <a href="/en/fiche-gorges-siagne">Gorges de la Siagne</a> round out a trip in this area between the Var and the Alpes-Maritimes.</p>
